from qlib import Q
C='value-appraisal'
Q(C,'basic',"Which appraisal approach is usually the most reliable for a single-family home?","Sales comparison approach",[("Income approach","The income approach is best for rental and investment property."),("Cost approach","The cost approach is best for new or special-purpose buildings."),("Gross rent multiplier","A GRM is a quick income method, mostly for rental homes.")],"Single-family homes → sales comparison approach","The **sales comparison (market data) approach** compares the subject home with similar homes that sold recently. Because buyers of homes compare homes this way, it is usually the most reliable approach for single-family homes.",5,["appraisal approaches"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"In the sales comparison approach, a comparable has a garage worth $10,000, but the subject property has no garage. The appraiser should:","Subtract $10,000 from the comparable's sale price",[("Add $10,000 to the comparable's sale price","You add only when the comparable lacks a feature the subject has."),("Add $10,000 to the subject's value","Adjustments are made to the comparables, never to the subject."),("Subtract $10,000 from the subject's value","Adjustments are made to the comparables, not the subject.")],"Comp better → subtract; comp poorer → add (CBS)","Adjustments are always made to the **comparable**. Remember **CBS: Comparable Better, Subtract**. The comparable has a garage the subject lacks, so it is better, and $10,000 is subtracted from its sale price.",5,["sales comparison"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which approach to value is most useful for a church or a new fire station?","Cost approach",[("Sales comparison approach","Churches and fire stations rarely sell, so few comparables exist."),("Income approach","These buildings do not produce rental income."),("Gross rent multiplier","A GRM needs rental income.")],"Special-purpose or new buildings → cost approach","The **cost approach** estimates the cost to replace or reproduce the building, subtracts **depreciation**, and adds the **land value**. It is best for new buildings and **special-purpose** properties that rarely sell and produce no income.",4,["cost approach"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A busy highway was built next to a residential street, lowering home values. This loss of value is:","External (economic) obsolescence",[("Physical deterioration","Physical deterioration is wear and tear on the building itself."),("Functional obsolescence","Functional obsolescence is an outdated design or feature inside the property."),("Curable depreciation","External obsolescence is almost always incurable because it is outside the owner's control.")],"Loss from outside the property = external obsolescence","The three kinds of **depreciation** are physical deterioration, **functional obsolescence** (poor design, outdated features), and **external (economic) obsolescence** (factors outside the property, such as a new highway or a factory). External obsolescence is usually incurable.",5,["depreciation"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A four-bedroom home has only one bathroom. This is an example of:","Functional obsolescence",[("External obsolescence","External obsolescence comes from outside the property."),("Physical deterioration","Physical deterioration is wear and tear, such as a worn roof."),("Economic life","Economic life is the period a building adds value; it is not a type of depreciation.")],"Poor design or layout = functional obsolescence","**Functional obsolescence** is a loss in value from poor design or outdated features, such as too few bathrooms for the number of bedrooms, or a bedroom you can reach only through another bedroom.",4,["depreciation"])
Q(C,'basic',"The principle of value stating that a buyer will not pay more for a property than the cost of an equally desirable substitute is:","Substitution",[("Conformity","Conformity says value is highest when homes are similar to each other."),("Contribution","Contribution is about how much one feature adds to value."),("Regression","Regression is when a better home loses value among poorer homes.")],"Substitution = basis of sales comparison","The principle of **substitution** says a buyer will not pay more than the cost of an equally good substitute. It is the basis of the sales comparison approach.",4,["principles of value"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"The most expensive house in a neighborhood of much smaller homes is worth less than it would be in a neighborhood of similar homes. This illustrates:","Regression",[("Progression","Progression is when a lower-priced home gains value from better homes around it."),("Anticipation","Anticipation is value based on expected future benefits."),("Plottage","Plottage is the added value from combining lots.")],"Best house in the neighborhood → regression","Under **regression**, a better property loses value when surrounded by lower-quality properties. **Progression** is the opposite: a modest home gains value from nicer homes around it.",4,["principles of value"])
Q(C,'basic',"Which statement about market value and market price is correct?","Market price is what a property actually sold for, which may differ from market value",[("They are always the same amount","They can differ, for example in a forced sale or a sale between relatives."),("Market value is the amount the seller asks","The asking price is the list price, not market value."),("Market price is set by the appraiser","An appraiser estimates value; buyers and sellers set the price.")],"Price = what was paid; value = most probable price","**Market value** is the most probable price a property should bring in a competitive, open market with informed buyers and sellers acting without pressure. **Market price** is what was actually paid, which may be different.",4,["value"])
Q(C,'advanced',"An investor wants a 10% capitalization rate. A building has net operating income of $45,000 a year. What value does the income approach indicate?","$450,000",[("$4,500","This divides by 10 instead of 0.10."),("$49,500","This adds 10% to the income instead of capitalizing it."),("$405,000","This subtracts 10% from $450,000 by mistake.")],"Value = NOI ÷ cap rate","Use the income formula:\n\n1. Value = Net operating income ÷ Cap rate.\n2. $45,000 ÷ 0.10 = **$450,000**.\n3. Check: $450,000 × 0.10 = $45,000.",5,["income approach","math"])
Q(C,'advanced',"In the income approach, net operating income (NOI) is found by subtracting which of the following from effective gross income?","Operating expenses such as taxes, insurance, and maintenance",[("Mortgage principal and interest payments","Debt service is not an operating expense and is not subtracted to find NOI."),("Depreciation for income tax purposes","Tax depreciation is not an operating expense."),("Vacancy and collection losses only","Vacancy is subtracted earlier, from potential gross income, to find effective gross income.")],"NOI = EGI − operating expenses (not debt service)","1. Potential gross income − vacancy and collection loss = **effective gross income**.\n2. Effective gross income − **operating expenses** = **NOI**.\n\nMortgage payments (debt service) and income tax depreciation are not operating expenses.",4,["income approach"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A home rents for $1,500 a month. Similar homes sold for about 120 times their monthly rent. What is the estimated value using the gross rent multiplier?","$180,000",[("$18,000","This drops a zero from the result."),("$150,000","This uses 100 instead of 120 as the multiplier."),("$1,620","This adds the multiplier to the rent instead of multiplying.")],"Value = monthly rent × GRM","1. Value = Monthly rent × Gross rent multiplier.\n2. $1,500 × 120 = **$180,000**.\n3. Check: $180,000 ÷ $1,500 = 120.",4,["GRM","math"])
Q(C,'basic',"A licensee prepares a report comparing a seller's home to recent nearby sales to help set a listing price. This is a:","Comparative market analysis (CMA)",[("Appraisal","An appraisal is done by a licensed or certified appraiser."),("Assessment","An assessment is the value set by the tax assessor."),("Title search","A title search examines ownership records.")],"CMA = licensee's price opinion, not an appraisal","A **comparative market analysis (CMA)** compares a home with recently sold, pending, and active listings to help set a price. It is not an appraisal, and licensees must not present it as one. A **broker price opinion (BPO)** is a similar estimate often requested by lenders.",4,["CMA"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Which of the following is NOT one of the four characteristics that give a property value?","Location in a flood zone",[("Demand","Demand (desire backed by ability to pay) is one of the four."),("Utility","Utility (usefulness) is one of the four."),("Transferability","Transferability is one of the four.")],"Value needs DUST: Demand, Utility, Scarcity, Transferability","The four characteristics of value are **D**emand, **U**tility, **S**carcity, and **T**ransferability (**DUST**). A flood zone location may affect value, but it is not one of the four characteristics.",3,["value"],lock=True)
C='contracts'
Q(C,'basic',"A contract in which both parties exchange promises, such as a purchase agreement, is a:","Bilateral contract",[("Unilateral contract","In a unilateral contract only one party makes a promise."),("Implied contract","An implied contract is created by actions, not words."),("Void contract","A void contract has no legal effect.")],"Promise for a promise = bilateral","In a **bilateral contract**, each party promises to do something: the seller promises to sell and the buyer promises to buy. In a **unilateral contract**, only one side promises, such as an option or an open listing.",5,["contract types"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Under the Statute of Frauds, a contract for the sale of real estate must be:","In writing to be enforceable",[("Recorded to be valid","Recording is not required for a contract to be enforceable."),("Notarized to be valid","Notarization is generally not required for a purchase contract."),("Approved by a court","No court approval is needed.")],"Real estate sale contract → must be in writing","The **Statute of Frauds** requires certain contracts, including contracts to sell real estate, to be **in writing** and signed to be enforceable. An oral agreement to sell land generally cannot be enforced in court.",5,["statute of frauds"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A buyer makes an offer, and the seller changes the closing date and signs. What is the legal effect?","The seller has rejected the offer and made a counteroffer",[("A binding contract is formed with the new date","A change is not acceptance; it creates a counteroffer."),("The original offer remains open for the buyer","A counteroffer ends the original offer."),("The buyer must accept the new date","The buyer is free to accept or reject the counteroffer.")],"Any change = counteroffer; original offer is dead","Acceptance must match the offer exactly. Any change is a **counteroffer**, which **rejects** the original offer. The buyer may now accept, reject, or counter the counteroffer.",5,["offer and acceptance"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A contract signed by a person who was a minor at the time is generally:","Voidable by the minor",[("Void from the start","It is not void; the minor may choose to keep or cancel it."),("Valid and enforceable against the minor","The minor can cancel it, so it is not fully enforceable against the minor."),("Unenforceable by either party","The adult party is bound if the minor chooses to keep the contract.")],"Minor's contract = voidable by the minor","A contract with a minor is usually **voidable** by the minor: the minor may cancel it, but the adult cannot. A **void** contract (for example, one for an illegal purpose) has no legal effect at all.",4,["contract validity"])
Q(C,'basic',"Which of the following is an essential element of a valid contract?","Consideration",[("Recording","Recording is not an element of a valid contract."),("Earnest money","Earnest money is common but not required for validity."),("A real estate agent's signature","Agents do not need to sign for a contract to be valid.")],"Valid contract: competent parties, offer & acceptance, consideration, legal purpose","Essential elements of a valid contract include **competent parties**, **offer and acceptance** (mutual agreement), **consideration** (something of value), and a **legal purpose**. Real estate sale contracts must also be **in writing**.",5,["contract elements"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A contract in which all parties have fully performed their duties is:","Executed",[("Executory","Executory means some duties are still to be done."),("Voidable","Voidable means a party can cancel it."),("Unilateral","Unilateral means only one party made a promise.")],"Executed = done; executory = not yet done","An **executed** contract has been fully performed, such as a sale after closing. An **executory** contract still has duties left to perform, such as a purchase agreement before closing.",4,["contract performance"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A buyer pays a seller $2,000 for the right to buy a property for $300,000 any time in the next 90 days. The seller cannot sell to anyone else during that time. This is:","An option contract",[("A right of first refusal","A right of first refusal only lets the holder match another buyer's offer."),("A land contract","A land contract is an installment sale with the seller financing."),("A lease","No right to occupy the property was given.")],"Option = optionee may buy; optionor must sell if exercised","In an **option contract**, the owner (**optionor**) gives the **optionee** the right, but not the duty, to buy at a set price within a set time in exchange for consideration. It is unilateral until the optionee exercises it.",4,["options"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which contract clause makes a purchase agreement depend on the buyer getting a loan?","Financing contingency",[("Time is of the essence","This clause requires that deadlines be met exactly."),("Liquidated damages","This clause sets in advance the damages for a breach."),("Acceleration clause","This is a loan clause that makes the whole debt due on default.")],"Contingency = condition that must be met","A **contingency** is a condition that must be met before the contract becomes binding. A **financing contingency** lets the buyer cancel if a loan cannot be obtained on the stated terms. Other common ones are inspection and appraisal contingencies.",5,["contingencies"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A purchase agreement says the seller may keep the earnest money as full damages if the buyer defaults. This is a:","Liquidated damages clause",[("Specific performance clause","Specific performance is a court order to carry out the contract."),("Rescission clause","Rescission undoes the contract and returns parties to their original positions."),("Novation","A novation replaces one contract or party with a new one.")],"Earnest money as agreed damages = liquidated damages","**Liquidated damages** are an amount agreed on in advance as the full damages for a breach. Keeping the earnest money is a common example. Other remedies for breach include **specific performance** and suing for **compensatory damages**.",4,["remedies"])
Q(C,'advanced',"After signing a purchase contract, the buyer holds an interest in the property before closing that is called:","Equitable title",[("Legal title","Legal title stays with the seller until the deed is delivered at closing."),("Fee simple absolute","The buyer does not own the property in fee until closing."),("A leasehold","The buyer is not a tenant.")],"Signed sales contract → buyer has equitable title","Once a valid purchase contract is signed, the buyer has **equitable title**, an interest that courts will protect. **Legal title** passes when the deed is delivered and accepted at closing.",4,["sales contract"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A contract with \"time is of the essence\" language means that:","Deadlines in the contract must be met exactly",[("The contract can be completed whenever convenient","The clause means the opposite."),("The closing must happen within 30 days","The clause does not set any specific number of days."),("Only the buyer must meet deadlines","Both parties must meet the deadlines.")],"Time is of the essence = deadlines are strict","When a contract says **time is of the essence**, the dates and deadlines are strict. A party who misses one may be in **breach**, and the other party may be allowed to cancel.",4,["contract performance"])
Q(C,'basic',"Which of the following best describes earnest money?","A deposit the buyer makes to show good faith when making an offer",[("The broker's commission paid at listing","Commission is paid to the broker, usually at closing."),("The lender's loan origination fee","An origination fee is a lender charge."),("A fee the seller pays to list the home","Listing fees are not earnest money.")],"Earnest money = buyer's good-faith deposit","**Earnest money** is a deposit from the buyer that shows serious intent. It is usually held in a broker's **escrow (trust) account** or by an escrow or title company, and is applied toward the price at closing.",4,["earnest money"])
C='agency'
Q(C,'basic',"In an agency relationship, the person who hires the agent is the:","Principal (client)",[("Customer","A customer is a third party the agent works with but does not represent."),("Subagent","A subagent is an agent of the agent."),("Fiduciary","The agent, not the person hiring, is the fiduciary.")],"Principal = client; agent owes fiduciary duties","The **principal** (client) hires the **agent** and is owed fiduciary duties. A **customer** is a party the agent works with but does not represent, such as a buyer working with the seller's agent.",5,["agency basics"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which of the following is one of the fiduciary duties an agent owes to a client?","Confidentiality",[("Guaranteeing the sale price","No agent can guarantee a price."),("Disclosing the client's lowest acceptable price to buyers","That would break the duties of loyalty and confidentiality."),("Giving legal advice about the deed","Giving legal advice is the unauthorized practice of law.")],"Fiduciary duties: OLD CAR","The common fiduciary duties are **O**bedience, **L**oyalty, **D**isclosure, **C**onfidentiality, **A**ccounting, and **R**easonable care (**OLD CAR**). The agent must keep the client's confidential information, such as the lowest acceptable price, private.",5,["fiduciary duties"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A seller's agent tells a buyer customer that the basement floods after heavy rain. The agent:","Did the right thing, because agents must disclose known material defects to all parties",[("Broke the duty of loyalty to the seller","Agents must disclose known material defects to buyers even when representing the seller."),("Should have told the buyer only after closing","The disclosure must be made before the buyer is committed."),("Needed the buyer's written permission first","No permission is needed to disclose a material defect.")],"Material defects must be disclosed to customers too","Agents owe customers **honesty and fair dealing**. A known **material defect**, such as a flooding basement, must be disclosed to buyers, even when the agent represents the seller. Hiding it can be misrepresentation or fraud.",5,["duties to customers"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A broker represents both the buyer and the seller in the same transaction with both parties' informed written consent. This is:","Dual agency",[("Single agency","In single agency the broker represents only one party."),("Subagency","Subagency is when another broker's agent works for the client through the listing broker."),("Undisclosed agency","Dual agency must be disclosed and agreed to, so it is not undisclosed.")],"Both sides with consent = dual agency","**Dual agency** exists when one broker represents both buyer and seller. It is allowed only where state law permits and with the **informed written consent** of both parties. Undisclosed dual agency is illegal. Some states use designated agency or transaction brokerage instead.",4,["types of agency"])
Q(C,'basic',"A seller signs a listing agreement with a broker. Who is the agent?","The broker",[("The seller","The seller is the principal."),("The buyer","The buyer is a customer unless represented by another agent."),("The multiple listing service","The MLS is a sharing service, not the agent.")],"Listing agreement: broker = agent, seller = principal","A listing agreement creates an **express agency**. The **broker** is the agent and the **seller** is the principal. Salespersons work on the broker's behalf.",4,["agency basics"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"Which event will terminate an agency relationship?","The death of the principal",[("The agent finding a buyer who later backs out","A failed sale alone does not end the listing."),("A drop in market prices","Market changes do not end the agency."),("The seller painting the house","Repairs do not end the agency.")],"Death, completion, expiration, mutual agreement end agency","An agency ends by **completion** of its purpose, **expiration** of its term, **mutual agreement**, **death or incapacity** of either party, **destruction** of the property, bankruptcy, or by **revocation or renunciation** (which may lead to damages).",4,["termination of agency"])
Q(C,'advanced',"Without the seller's permission, the listing agent tells a buyer, \"The seller is going through a divorce and will take much less.\" The agent violated the duty of:","Loyalty and confidentiality",[("Accounting","Accounting is about handling and reporting money."),("Obedience","Obedience is about following the client's lawful instructions."),("Reasonable care","Reasonable care is about competence; this is a breach of loyalty and confidentiality.")],"Revealing the seller's motivation = breach of loyalty","An agent must put the client's interest first (**loyalty**) and keep the client's private information, such as motivation and lowest price, **confidential**. Telling the buyer harms the seller's bargaining position.",4,["fiduciary duties"])
Q(C,'advanced',"A buyer's agent finds that the buyer could get a better price on another home listed by the agent's own brokerage, which would pay the agent a higher commission. The agent should:","Act in the buyer's best interest and disclose any conflict of interest",[("Push the home that pays more","This would put the agent's interest ahead of the client's."),("Hide the commission difference from the buyer","Conflicts of interest must be disclosed."),("Stop working with the buyer without notice","Abandoning the client is not a proper solution.")],"Client's interest first; disclose conflicts","Loyalty requires the agent to put the **client's interest** ahead of the agent's own. Any **conflict of interest** or self-interest that could affect the client must be disclosed.",3,["fiduciary duties","conflicts of interest"])
Q(C,'intermediate',"A buyer works with a salesperson but never signs any agreement. Over time, the salesperson begins acting like the buyer's agent. An agency may be created by:","Implication from the parties' conduct",[("Recording a deed","Recording a deed does not create agency."),("Escheat","Escheat concerns property passing to the state."),("Ratification by the seller","The seller cannot create the buyer's agency.")],"Agency can be express or implied by conduct","Agency may be **express** (a written or oral agreement) or **implied** from the parties' conduct. Licensees should make relationships clear in writing to avoid an **accidental (implied) agency**.",3,["creation of agency"])
Q(C,'basic',"An agent's duty to account for all money and property belonging to others is the duty of:","Accounting",[("Obedience","Obedience means following lawful instructions."),("Disclosure","Disclosure means sharing relevant information with the client."),("Loyalty","Loyalty means putting the client's interest first.")],"Accounting = handle others' money properly","The duty of **accounting** requires the agent to safeguard and report all money and documents belonging to others, such as earnest money, and never mix them with the agent's own funds (**commingling**).",4,["fiduciary duties"])
